From Raw Data to Business Decision: Supermarket Sales Analysis

Business Problem
A supermarket has sales data but lacks a clear understanding of what is driving its sales performance. Without analyzing sales trends, product categories, branch performance, and customer purchasing patterns, it may be difficult to identify opportunities for improvement and make informed business decisions.
Objective
The objective of this project was to analyze supermarket sales data using Microsoft Excel to identify sales trends, evaluate product and branch performance, understand customer and payment patterns, and translate the findings into actionable business recommendations.
Tools Used
•	Microsoft Excel: Data cleaning, data validation, formulas, PivotTables, charts, dashboard development, and slicers.
•	Excel formulas: TRIM, DATEVALUE, and TIMEVALUE to address inconsistent text, dates, and time formats.
•	Excel PivotTables: To summarize sales and compare performance across different business dimensions.
•	Excel dashboard: To present key performance indicators and interactive visualizations.
Dataset
This project uses a simulated supermarket sales dataset containing approximately 199 sales records across three months, from January to March 2026.
The dataset includes sales dates, branches, customer types, products, product categories, quantities, prices, discounts, costs, and payment methods.
Note: This is a learning project using simulated data, not data obtained from a real supermarket.


Process
1.	Business Problem Definition: Established the business question and identified the key areas requiring analysis.
2.	Data Understanding: Examined the dataset structure, fields, and potential data quality issues.
3.	Data Cleaning: Addressed inconsistent text formatting, extra spaces, date and time formats, and missing values.
4.	Data Validation: Reviewed the cleaned data to ensure corrections were appropriate for analysis.
5.	Exploratory Data Analysis: Used PivotTables and Excel formulas to investigate sales trends, product categories, branch performance, customer types, and payment methods.
6.	Dashboard Development: Built an interactive Excel dashboard featuring KPI summaries, charts, and slicers for Branch and Customer Type.
7.	Business Insights: Interpreted the findings and developed recommendations for further investigation and potential business action.
Dashboard
The dashboard provides an overview of sales performance and allows users to explore results by branch and customer type.
Key Insights
•	Total Sales: The dataset recorded ₦2,663,691.98 in total sales.
•	Gross Profit: Total gross profit was ₦783,901.98, representing a gross profit margin of approximately 29.44%.
•	Monthly Performance: February recorded the highest sales at ₦1,040,954.75, an increase of approximately 61.2% compared with January.
•	Product Category Performance: Grocery was the highest-selling category, generating ₦673,063.95 in sales.
•	Branch Performance: Challenge recorded the highest branch sales at ₦1,013,387.45, while Ring Road recorded ₦702,826.40.
•	Top-Selling Product: Cooking Oil was the leading individual product by sales, generating ₦224,563.20.
•	Payment Methods: Transfer recorded the highest sales value among the payment methods analyzed.
Recommendations
Based on the findings, the following actions could help guide further business investigation:
1.	Review product performance: Investigate the products contributing most to Grocery sales and gross profit, and monitor stock availability.
2.	Investigate branch differences: Compare transaction volumes, product mix, customer patterns, and other relevant operating factors across branches.
3.	Examine monthly changes: Investigate the factors associated with February's sales increase and March's subsequent decline.
4.	Monitor leading products: Review Cooking Oil sales patterns and stock availability to understand whether its performance is consistent over time.
5.	Explore customer behavior: Compare purchase frequency and average transaction value between Members and Normal customers before changing customer strategies.
6.	Review payment patterns: Use the payment-method findings to investigate transaction preferences and operational requirements.
These recommendations are proposed actions based on the observed patterns. Further investigation would be required to establish the causes of the differences and evaluate the effectiveness of any intervention.
What I Learned
Through this project, I practiced the end-to-end data analysis workflow, including:
•	Identifying a business problem and framing analytical questions.
•	Detecting and correcting data quality issues.
•	Using Excel formulas and PivotTables to analyze data.
•	Building an interactive dashboard.
•	Translating analytical findings into business implications.
•	Communicating results while distinguishing observed patterns from unverified assumptions.
